# SYNOPSIS

```text
userboot.next=bin/launcher [launcher.until=service]
```

# DESCRIPTION

The launcher is the program userboot starts.
It reads every manifest in bootfs's `etc/manifests/` (manifest(6)), checks them against each other, and starts each service in a job and a process of its own, given only what its manifest grants: its arguments, its `/env`, the trees it uses at `/svc` and where it mounts them, and any hardware, boot data or debuglog its manifest asks for.
A service starts after every service it uses has answered on its tree; services marked `early` start first.
Any error in a manifest stops the launch, with its file and line.

A process's `/svc` holds only the services it uses, each forwarding to the service's current process, so after a restart opening the path again reaches the new one.
Each `mount` of a service is given with a forwarder of the launcher's, which it points at each new instance: when the service restarts, the process's namespace finds its channel closed and reconnects through the forwarder, and a child given that namespace gets a forwarder of its own.
What was open in the old instance stays closed.
A service that ends is restarted as its manifest's `restart` says; one restarted 5 times within 30 seconds is left failed.

Given `launcher.until=<service>` on the kernel's command line, the launcher runs until that service ends, and exits with its exit code: how a boot test runs a client to its end.
Without it, it supervises the services for good.

# FILES

: `/svc/launcher/status`
  A line for each service: its name, its state (`not started`, `starting`, `running`, `failed`, or `exited` and its code) and how many times it has been restarted, as `restarts <n>`.
: `/svc/launcher/memory`
  A line for each service, `<name> processes <n> private <p> shared <s> scaled <x>`, the bytes its processes and those it started hold, then the launcher's own as `launcher`.
  A service's resident memory is its private and scaled bytes.
: `/svc/launcher/ctl`
  Takes `kill <service>`, which ends the service's process as a crash would; its restart policy then applies.
: `etc/manifests/*.manifest`
  In bootfs, the services to run.

